      "role": "user",
      "content": "interesting. so when these changes happen, like the new shops and higher rents coming in, what does that usually do to the people already living there? like, what kind of residents typically move into these revitalized areas vs the ones who end up leaving?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Typically, the people who move into these revitalized areas are young, educated, and affluent, often from the suburbs or other gentrifying neighborhoods. They tend to be professionals, artists, or entrepreneurs who are drawn to the area's trendy vibe and desirability. In contrast, the existing residents, often long-time low-income and minority families, are often priced out of their own neighborhoods and forced to relocate to outer-ring suburbs or other areas with lower property values. This displacement can lead to a loss of community and cultural identity for the original residents.",
